    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "Contrary to a statement in the lead article in the April 7, 1973, issue cautioning the clinician to wait 6 weeks or longer after abortion or delivery before inserting an IUD, the author cites a World Health Organization report which says that risks of perforation or translocation appear to be the smallest right after delivery or 3 or more months postpartum. The study suggests that the highest risk is between 4 to 8 weeks after delivery. The author also takes issue with a statement relating to the possible increased incidence of ectopic pregnancy among IUD wearers. He says that calculations of the incidence of ectopic pregnancies per woman-month of exposure show that the number os such pregnancies is possible reduced by the IUD, but not as much as the incidenct of uterine pregnancies."
    }
  },
